1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:05,800
Uh, look at this weather! I'm really\h
looking\hforward to the summer holidays.

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:09,200
I just\hneed to get away and lie on a beach.

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:14,160
Hello, this is Georgie from BBC Learning\h
English. Today we're looking at phrasal\h\h

4
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:23,200
verbs for 'travel'. Let the\h
learning plane 'take off'!

5
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,800
If you 'look forward to' something,\h\h

6
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:29,960
it means 'you're anticipating something\h
good or exciting happening in the future'.

7
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:35,000
'He's really looking forward to\h
his summer holiday by the beach.'

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,480
'get away' in the context of\h
travel means 'to escape from\h\h

9
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:42,440
your usual responsibilities and go on holiday'.

10
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:48,640
'I'm feeling burnt out. I'd love to get away for\h
a few days and spend time in the countryside.'

11
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:52,880
If you 'pick someone up,' it means 'you\h
collect them and take them to another\h\h

12
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:58,840
location, usually in a vehicle like\h
a car'. This verb can be separated.

13
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:04,560
'The taxi picked the woman up from her\h
house and took her to the airport.'

14
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:09,840
'The taxi picked up the woman from her\h
house and took her to the airport.'

15
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:14,360
If you tell someone to 'hurry up,'\h
'you're demanding they go faster,\h\h

16
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:17,040
usually because you have limited time'.

17
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:20,640
'Hurry up! We're going to miss our flight!'

18
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:23,320
'set off' means 'to begin your journey'.

19
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:28,880
'Grandpa set off at 6am to make sure he\h
arrived at the airport early for his flight.'

20
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:33,120
'check in' is a common phrasal verb\h
for travel. It means 'to register\h\h

21
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:38,560
your arrival at an airport, at the\h
hotel, or some kind of facility'.

22
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:43,840
'After checking in at the hotel\h
reception, she gave them the room key.'

23
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:46,560
Finally, the plane 'takes off' when 'it leaves the\h\h

24
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:50,880
runway' and lands when it\h
arrives at the destination.

25
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:54,800
Remember, don't 'freak out', just\h
'come back' to BBC Learning English\h\h

